<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First World War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1914">1914</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as World War I, Great War</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First World War (1914–1918) was a global conflict between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allied Powers</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. It destroyed the Austro-Hungarian, German, Russian and Ottoman Empires and severely weakened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-dinar">Ali Dinar</a>'s alignment with the Ottomans during the war also led to the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-world-war.jpg" alt="First World War" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Collection DocAnciens/docpix.fr · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_de_Verdun_1916.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Argentina became the most prosperous and urbanised country in Latin America in the early twentieth century and, by some estimates, among the ten richest nations in the world by income per head before the <b>First World War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The assassination of the heir to the throne, Archduke Franz Ferdinand, in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914 by the Bosnian Serb nationalist Gavrilo Princip triggered the July Crisis that produced the <b>First World War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The structural causes lay in the <b>First World War</b>, which multiplied Britain's national debt, passed the role of the world's leading creditor to the United States and weakened the financial foundations on which imperial power depended.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>First World War</b> more than a million Indians served overseas in France, Mesopotamia, Gallipoli, Palestine and East Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>First World War</b> some 620,000 Canadians enlisted and about 60,000 died; the Canadian Corps won a celebrated victory at the Battle of Vimy Ridge in April 1917 and spearheaded the Hundred Days offensive of 1918, while the conscription crisis of 1917 split English and French Canada.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>First World War</b> about 416,000 Australians enlisted, all volunteers after conscription was rejected in referendums in 1916 and 1917, and more than 60,000 died.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
